#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
he_pelka_xgb_options.py
=======================
Tests four ways to incorporate XGBoost temperature/precipitation forecasts
into the Pelka & Musshoff (2013) hedging effectiveness framework.

Options tested
--------------
  1. Forecast-conditional hedging  — farmer buys the derivative only when
                                     XGBoost forecasts a bad accumulation year
  2. Hedge on forecast residuals   — derivative written on ε_t = actual - forecast
                                     (pure surprise insurance)
  3. XGBoost-informed strike       — strike = XGBoost forecast for that year
                                     instead of the long-run historical mean
  4. XGBoost-priced premium        — premium bootstrapped from forecast errors
                                     rather than from the raw index distribution

Timing assumption (cross-year periods)
---------------------------------------
  For harvest year t with a Nov-Jan temperature window:
    - Nov-Dec of year t-1: already observed (actual data) when farmer decides
    - Jan of year t:       XGBoost forecast
  The farmer makes her hedging decision on 1 January of the harvest year,
  at which point she has observed all autumn weather and XGBoost forecasts
  the remaining accumulation months.

Prerequisites
-------------
  - pelka_table1_tailored.csv  (from he_pelka2014.py — gives best periods)
  - Temperature PKLs:   Outputs/xgBoost_rolling_exports/
                        paper_xgb_region_{R}_calib_{Y}.pkl
  - Precipitation PKLs: Outputs/xgBoost_precip_rolling_exports/
                        paper_xgb_precip_region_{R}_calib_{Y}.pkl
  - EDA/region_avg.csv
  - EDA/region_avg_precip.csv
  - EDA/ble_tendre_hiver_yield_2000_2024_regions_mainland.csv
"""

